  "question": {
    "text": "The differential equation which represents the family of curves $y=c_1 e^{c_2 x}$, where $c_1$ and $c_2$ are arbitrary constants is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "TByyv",
      "text": "\n$y^{\\prime}=y^2$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ipwoX",
      "text": "\n$y^{\\prime \\prime}=y^{\\prime} y$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "5Y4go",
      "text": "\n$y y^{\\prime \\prime}=y^{\\prime}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ezE49",
      "text": "\n$y y^{\\prime \\prime}=\\left(y^{\\prime}\\right)^2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& y=c_1 e^{c_2 x} \\\\\n& y^{\\prime}=c_2 c_1 e^{c_2 x} \\\\\n& y^{\\prime}=c_2 y \\\\\n& y^{\\prime \\prime}=c_2 y^{\\prime}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFrom (2)\n$$\nc_2=\\frac{y^{\\prime}}{y}\n$$\nSo, $y^{\\prime \\prime}=\\frac{\\left(y^{\\prime}\\right)^2}{y} \\Rightarrow y y^{\\prime \\prime}=\\left(y^{\\prime}\\right)^2$",
    "image": null
  },
